{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED: task/sequencer-key-validation at exact head 707ddde86e3e5b8ce65b901a777af452f9a0836d, against request 1340f54f4cc6fe2effc15270c5715ae122293148 and promise 109d1391cd1cd2daab2c1544f08e079b41850c53. Worktree clean at that head; make vet, make test (12 packages) and go test -race all green.\n\nI re-ran the same sweep that raised the finding rather than reading the new tests and agreeing with them. All four named mutations now fail, and each fails in the test that claims the subject: the base64 canonical round-trip and the key-type check both fail TestValidateSSHPublicKeyRejectsAllowedSignersInjection, the VerifySSHCommit call fails TestVerifySSHCommitValidatesPublicKeyBeforeFormatting, and the GenerateSSHKey call fails TestGenerateSSHKeyValidatesKeygenOutput.\n\nThe VerifySSHCommit test is the best of the four and worth naming, because it turns the boundary's own behaviour against it. VerifySSHCommit trims the key when it builds the allowed-signers line, so a trailing-space key is one the formatter would silently normalise into something valid — which means without the explicit validation the call genuinely succeeds, and the assertion is pinned to a real difference rather than to an error string. It also verifies the canonical key first, so the refusal is a refusal and not a broken setup.\n\nRemoving the empty-body guard does not broaden acceptance. I checked the empty body and its neighbours directly: \"ssh-ed25519 \" and \"ssh-ed25519 AAAA\" are refused as algorithm mismatch by the wire parser, \"ssh-ed25519\", \" \", \"\" and a double space are refused as non-canonical, and \"ssh-ed25519 =\" and \"ssh-ed25519 A\" are refused as non-canonical base64. Every input the deleted clause used to catch is still refused; only the message differs. Dropping it was the right call over keeping a guard whose name overstated its coverage.\n\nOne thing I checked rather than assumed, because it is the shape I have been raising all round. TestGenerateSSHKeyValidatesKeygenOutput asserts only that an error occurs, and it fakes ssh-keygen through PATH — so a fake that could never produce a key at all would make the test pass while proving nothing. I ran the positive control: the same script emitting a canonical ed25519 key returns cleanly from GenerateSSHKey. The harness can succeed, so its refusal is genuine. Clean, but it is one line of setup away from vacuous, and a future edit to that script would not be caught by anything.\n\nScope is exactly what it claims: one line of gitstore.go and the test file. The envelope baseline is untouched.","body":{"branch":"task/sequencer-key-validation","head":"707ddde86e3e5b8ce65b901a777af452f9a0836d","verdict":"APPROVED"}}